              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Deck warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime (residential)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Electronics / parts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 years (residential)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Labour</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years (residential)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            A Canadian retailer’s listing for the older machine gives the same residential terms —
            lifetime frame, motor and deck, five years on parts and two on labour — and FitRated gave
            shorter light-commercial terms alongside them. The current US page lists only one set. TreadmillReviews.com, describing the touchscreen
            version, gives lifetime frame and motor, three years of parts and one of labour for
            residential use, and lifetime frame and motor, two years of parts and one of labour for
            light commercial use; Sole’s current page does not mention a commercial term.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical point is simple. Old reviews praising the TT8’s "lifetime deck" or "two
            years of in-home labour" describe a warranty you will not get on a new one bought today.
            If you are buying from a dealer, ask which version is in the box and get the warranty in
            writing. If you are buying second-hand, the console tells you which you are looking at,
            and you should assume no warranty transfers until Sole confirms otherwise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-deck-decline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Motor, Deck, Rollers and Decline</h2>
<p>
            Sole lists a 4.0 HP motor and does not say whether that is a continuous or peak rating.
            TreadmillReviews.com calls it 4.0 HP DC and FitRated, on the older version, 4.0 CHP. Treat
            it as a strong figure for a home treadmill, and remember that horsepower ratings are only
            comparable when brands rate them the same way; our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains continuous, peak and test ratings. Sole’s lifetime motor warranty says more about
            the motor than the number does.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of the drivetrain is where the TT8 earns its light-commercial label. Its 3-inch
            front and rear rollers are larger than those on many folding home treadmills, and a
            larger roller bends the belt less and spreads the load on its bearings. The
            running surface is 22 by 60 inches, the width and length most runners should look for;
            our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            covers why. Sole lists a 25 mm deck that can be flipped when one side wears, pre-coated
            with wax so that, in Sole’s words, it needs no lubrication. That is a real saving in
            maintenance, though the belt still needs to be kept clean, centred and correctly
            tensioned. The 400 lb rating is the highest Sole sells and one of the highest under
            $3,000; our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for heavy people</a>
            guide sets out why rated capacity matters.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Decline</h3>
          <p>
            Sole says the TT8 uses two incline motors to move the deck from 6 levels below flat to 15
            above it. No other current Sole declines, and among home treadmills, decline is rare
            enough to be the reason people choose this machine. It matters if you train for hilly
            road races or trail races with long descents, because running downhill loads the quads
            in a way flat running does not. Our guide to
            <a href="/what-is-treadmill-decline-good-for/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmill decline is good for</a>
            explains how to use it, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline roundup</a>
            compares the TT8 with the NordicTrack machines that go to -6%.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Console, SOLE+ App and Connectivity</h2>
<p>
            The current TT8 has a 15.6-inch, 1920 by 1080 touchscreen, the same size as the F85’s. Sole
            lists built-in Wi-Fi so workouts can stream without tethering a phone, the free SOLE+ app
            with no monthly subscription, Kinomap integration, Bluetooth FTMS and CSCS output for
            third-party apps, Bluetooth audio and speakers, a tablet holder, cooling fans and a
            10-watt wireless charging pad. Quick keys take speed to 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 9 or 12 mph and
            incline to 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 12 or 15, and there are speed and elevation controls in the
            handles, <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>pulse grips</a> and heart-rate control. Sole’s page does not include a chest strap or
            list named programs beyond these.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sole’s page carries a safety note worth repeating: the wireless charging pad emits a
            magnetic field, and anyone with an implanted medical device such as a pacemaker,
            defibrillator or insulin pump should consult a doctor before using the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two points from the one review of this version are worth knowing, with the caveat that its
            reviewer says they had not run on the TT8: that the current console has no USB charging
            port, only the wireless pad, and that mirroring an Android phone to the screen needs an
            extra app. Neither is a reason not to buy, but if you charge a phone or watch by cable
            during long runs, check the console before your return window closes. If you would rather
            have the machine change speed and incline for you during classes, that is what iFIT on a
            NordicTrack does; our
            <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart treadmill roundup</a>
            sets out the trade-off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owner-reports" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Independent Coverage Reports</h2>
<p>
            Independent coverage of the current touchscreen TT8 is thin: the one review we found that
            describes it says its reviewer had not used the machine, and others we found describe the
            older LCD version. So we separate what is consistent across versions from what is not.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Built heavily.</strong> Every source describes the TT8 as sturdy and heavy, and the 319 to 322 lb weight and non-folding frame are consistent across versions.</li>
            <li><strong>Does not fold.</strong> Both reviews list this as a drawback, along with a large footprint.</li>
            <li><strong>Expensive for walkers.</strong> TreadmillReviews.com calls it pricey and possibly overkill for walkers and casual runners; FitRated, on the older version, makes the same point indirectly by recommending it for runners.</li>
            <li><strong>No subscription needed.</strong> Both versions run on Sole’s free app and onboard controls.</li>
            <li><strong>An underwhelming fan.</strong> TreadmillReviews.com says so for the current version; we did not find it repeated elsewhere, so it is a single report.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We did not find independent measured data — speed-change times, noise readings or incline
            accuracy — for the current TT8. Until there is some, the strongest evidence about how it
            runs is the long record of the earlier versions on the same frame, and the lifetime
            frame and motor warranty. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">reading treadmill reviews</a>
            explains how to weigh single reports.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Space, Power and Delivery</h2>
<p>
            The TT8 is 81 inches long, 38 wide and 66 tall, about 21.4 square feet, and it stays that
            size because it does not fold. At 319 lb (367 lb boxed, in an 83 by 37 by 17-inch carton)
            it is a machine you place once. Leave clear space behind the deck — our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space guide</a>
            gives the margins — and add the deck height and a running bounce to the tallest user’s
            height when you measure the ceiling. Sole’s US page does not give a step-up height.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sole’s US page does not list electrical requirements for the TT8, so read the manual
            before delivery. As with any high-powered treadmill, plan on a dedicated grounded circuit
            and no extension lead; our
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">circuit guide</a>
            explains why. Sole’s 2026 F80 manual excludes garage use from its warranty, so if a garage
            is your plan, check what the TT8’s manual says before you order.
          </p>
          <p>
            Delivery is by LTL freight. Sole’s page lists standard curbside delivery at $99.99 in 10 to
            14 business days, room of choice for $200 more and room of choice with assembly for $350
            more, while a banner on the same page advertises free curbside shipping on every Sole
            machine. Ask which applies at checkout. Sole offers a 30-day satisfaction guarantee;
            read its returns page before you rely on it, because returning a 367 lb freight item is
            rarely free. Our
            <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">moving guide</a>
            covers getting it into a basement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. The Warranty, Read Properly</h2>
<p>
            Sole’s warranty block for the current TT8 reads: lifetime frame, lifetime motor, three
            years on the deck, three years on electronics, three years on wear items and parts, and
            one year on labour. The same page’s specification table leaves the labour line blank.
            We use one year, which matches the warranty block, the current-version review and Sole’s
            labour term on Sole’s other current treadmills. Do not confuse it with the 90 days
            shown on some Sole warranty tables: that is the cosmetic term, as Sole’s F80 and F85 pages
            show.
          </p>
          <p>
            One year of labour against lifetime frame and motor cover is the split to plan around.
            From year two, a covered part may arrive free while the technician’s visit to fit it does
            not, and on a non-folding 319 lb machine some jobs are not ones you will want to do
            yourself. Sole sells SOLE CARE+ protection plans at checkout; on 5 November its page
            showed two different sets of plan prices and coverage descriptions, so read the plan
            terms carefully before you buy one. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            explains how to judge an extended plan.
          </p>
          <p>
            Safety record: we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database on
            5 November 2026 for Sole and Sole Fitness and found no Sole treadmill recall. Our
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole Fitness guide</a>
            covers the company and its service record, and our
            <a href="/sole-vs-nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole vs NordicTrack</a>
            comparison sets the two brands’ warranties side by side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. If the TT8 Stays Sold Out: TT8 vs F85, F80 and NordicTrack X16</h2>
<p>
            Sole has shown the TT8 as sold out on at least two checks three weeks apart, on 13 October
            and 5 November 2026. You can wait, you can look for a dealer with stock, or you can buy
            one of these. Prices are those shown on soletreadmills.com and NordicTrack’s checkout
            page on 5 November 2026.
          </p>
