              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Running surface</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 x 60 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4.0 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.5 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.25 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4.25 CHP</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Grade range</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">-6 to 15 levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0-15 levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0-15 levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">-6% to 40%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Max user weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">350 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">350 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folds</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price and stock</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,899.99, sold out</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,199.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,599.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,699, in stock</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole F85.</strong> The closest Sole: the same running surface and 15.6-inch
            touchscreen, a folding frame, and $700 less than the TT8’s list price. It gives up decline,
            half a horsepower and 50 lb of rating. If you do not need decline and weigh well under
            350 lb, it is the sensible buy today; our
            <a href="/sole-f85-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F85 review</a>
            sets out its versions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole F80.</strong> $1,300 less than the TT8, with a 20-inch belt, a 3.25 HP motor
            and a 10.1-inch touchscreen. It suits runners of ordinary build who want Sole’s frame and
            motor cover without the TT8’s size; our
            <a href="/sole-f80-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F80 review</a>
            covers it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack X16.</strong> If decline is the reason you wanted the TT8, the X16
            declines to -6% and climbs to 40%, with the same 400 lb rating and 22 by 60-inch belt, for
            $800 more than the TT8’s list price. Most of its screen depends on a paid iFIT membership,
            and its parts cover is two years; our
            <a href="/nordictrack-x24-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack X24 review</a>
            covers the same machine with a larger screen. The full Sole range is ranked in our
            <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best Sole treadmills</a>
            guide, and other heavy non-folding machines in our
            <a href="/best-non-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">non-folding roundup</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If you find one elsewhere</h3>
          <p>
            Dealers and second-hand sellers do have TT8s. Before you pay, ask which console it has,
            check the price against Sole’s $2,899.99 list, and get the warranty terms in writing. A
            dealer price above Sole’s list for a machine that Sole itself will sell you when stock
            returns is rarely worth paying. For a used TT8, our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            covers the belt, deck and motor checks, and the incline motors are worth running through
            their full range, decline included.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy the Sole TT8</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want decline from Sole.</strong> It is the only current Sole that
            goes downhill, and it does so on a heavy, stable, non-folding frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are a heavy user or run high mileage.</strong> A 400 lb rating,
            4.0 HP motor, 3-inch rollers and a reversible deck are built for hard use, and the
            lifetime frame and motor cover backs them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the F85 instead</strong> if you need a treadmill now, want it to fold, or do not
            need decline. You keep the deck size and the screen and save $700.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere</strong> if you mainly walk, where the TT8 is more machine than you
            need, or if you want classes to control the treadmill, where iFIT-based NordicTrack
            machines are designed for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Whatever you buy, check the version.</strong> The current TT8’s warranty is shorter
            than the older one’s, so do not rely on a review or a listing that describes the LCD model.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Sole TT8 treadmill good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On its published figures, yes, for heavy users and runners who want decline. Sole lists a 4.0 HP motor, a 22 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 15 incline and 6 decline levels, a 400 lb rating, 3-inch rollers and a lifetime frame and motor warranty. Independent coverage of the current touchscreen version is thin, and we have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Sole TT8 discontinued?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sole has not said so. Its US store still lists the TT8 at $2,899.99, but it was marked sold out on 5 November 2026, as it was on 13 October. We do not know when stock will return. If you need a treadmill now, the in-stock Sole F85 is the closest alternative.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty does the Sole TT8 have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sole’s warranty block for the current TT8 gives lifetime on the frame and motor, three years each on the deck, electronics and wear parts, and one year on labour. Older versions carried a lifetime deck, five years of parts and two of labour, so reviews describing those terms are out of date.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Sole TT8 have decline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Sole lists the grade as -6 to 15 levels, driven by two incline motors, and it is the only current Sole treadmill with decline. For deeper incline with the same -6% decline, the NordicTrack X16 and X24 go to 40%.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Sole TT8 vs F85: what is the difference?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both have a 22 by 60-inch running surface and a 15.6-inch touchscreen. The TT8 adds decline, a 4.0 HP motor against 3.5 HP and a 400 lb rating against 350 lb, but does not fold. On 5 November 2026 the F85 was $2,199.99 and in stock; the TT8 was listed at $2,899.99 and sold out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Sole TT8 fold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. It is a non-folding treadmill measuring 81 by 38 by 66 inches and weighing 319 lb. If you need to fold the machine away, the Sole F85 and F80 fold.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Sole TT8</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The TT8 is a good machine with a confusing history. These are the errors that cost buyers money or frustration.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a review of the older version</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many TT8 reviews describe the 10.1-inch LCD model with a lifetime deck and two years of labour. The current touchscreen model has three years on the deck and one on labour. Check which version any source describes.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying over list to a reseller while Sole is sold out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sole lists the TT8 at $2,899.99. A third-party price above that, for a machine Sole will sell when stock returns, is rarely worth paying unless you need it urgently and the warranty is confirmed in writing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it for walking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The TT8’s motor, rollers and 400 lb frame are built for hard running. If you mainly walk, a folding Sole or a cheaper machine does the job for far less.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting it does not fold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">At 81 by 38 inches and 319 lb, the TT8 stays where it is installed. Measure the room, the doorway and the ceiling before you order, and plan the delivery route for a 367 lb box.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

